# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# OpsHub — LOCAL DEVELOPMENT environment
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# From a clean clone, `cp .env.example .env` is the ONLY setup this file needs. Every value below boots the API,
# the worker and the web dev server against `docker compose -f docker-compose.dev.yml up -d` (README → "Local stack").
# `test/env-example.spec.ts` parses this file against the real schemas and the dev tooling on every CI run, and the
# "local quickstart" job boots the stack from it, so it cannot drift quietly.
#
# SECRETS HERE ARE LOCAL PLACEHOLDERS (`local-dev-only-…`), not secrets. They only have to be 32+ characters. Never
# paste a real or deployed value into this file or into `.env`; to rotate a local one:  openssl rand -base64 48
#
# WHO READS WHAT. One `.env` serves two processes whose schemas differ on purpose:
#   [both]    read by the API and the worker.
#   [API]     the API only. The worker's schema REFUSES TO START with these set (a worker holding the session secret
#             could forge a cookie), so `pnpm start:dev:worker` blanks them for the worker process.
#   [WORKER]  the worker only. The API's schema refuses these, so `pnpm start:dev` blanks them for the API process.
#   An empty value counts as unset, and a variable already in the environment wins over this file: that is how the
#   blanking works. Deployed environments set variables per task and never use this file.
#
# PORTS (all offset from rova's defaults so both stacks run side by side; see README → "Local stack"):
#   API 3001 · web 5173 · Postgres 5433 · Valkey 6380 · LocalStack 4567 · OTel collector 4319 (metrics 8890)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── Process ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# [both] `development` is what allows the passwordless test login and the demo seed; `production` refuses both.
NODE_ENV=development
# [API] 3001, NOT 3000: apps/web/vite.config.ts proxies /v1 and /api/auth to http://localhost:3001, and the web
# E2E in CI uses 3001. A different port here makes every request from the SPA fail with a 502 from the proxy.
PORT=3001
HOST=0.0.0.0
# [API] The Vite dev server's origin (CORS allow-list).
CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173
# [both] The origin the BROWSER uses: the SPA. Used in email links, as identity's base URL and trusted origin, and
# for the Microsoft redirect URI `${APP_URL}/api/auth/callback/microsoft`.
APP_URL=http://localhost:5173

# ── Database (PostgreSQL 18) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# [both] 5433 is the host port docker-compose.dev.yml maps (rova's stack owns 5432). Supply EITHER a complete
# DATABASE_URL (this file, CI) OR the discrete parts DATABASE_HOST/PORT/NAME/USER/PASSWORD, which deployed
# environments use so the password comes straight from the RDS-managed secret. See db/database-url.ts.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://opshub:opshub@localhost:5433/opshub
DATABASE_POOL_MIN=2
DATABASE_POOL_MAX=20
# Privileged connection for db:migrate, the e2e reset and db:roles:enable. Unset locally, where DATABASE_URL is
# already the superuser; set in every deployed environment (docs/runbooks/db-role-least-privilege.md).
# DATABASE_MIGRATION_URL=postgres://opshub:opshub@localhost:5433/opshub
# Passwords for the least-privilege roles, consumed by `pnpm db:roles:enable` at cutover. [A-Za-z0-9_-], 24+ chars.
# DATABASE_APP_PASSWORD=
# DATABASE_WORKER_PASSWORD=
# `pnpm db:migrate` (and `pnpm db:seed`) with this on also load the DEMO FIXTURES: one login-able employee per
# system role (admin@opshub.local, hr@opshub.local, employee@opshub.local, …), with their identity users. The test
# login below signs in as one of them. Without it the migrate step seeds only the RBAC catalogue and every
# sign-in is refused. Refused by the migrator whenever NODE_ENV=production.
SEED_ON_DEPLOY=true

# ── Auth (identity 8: Better Auth, `staff` preset, Microsoft Entra) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Sessions are server-side rows behind ONE opaque cookie (`__Secure-opshub.session_token`); no token reaches the
# browser. The SPA and the API must be same-origin: locally the Vite proxy provides that for /api/auth/* and /v1/*;
# deployed, it is the Cloudflare Pages Function in apps/web/functions/.
#
# [API] Signs and encrypts identity's cookies and secrets. 32+ characters.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=local-dev-only-better-auth-secret-0123456789
# [both] Signs fastify-cookie. 32+ characters.
COOKIE_SECRET=local-dev-only-cookie-secret-0123456789abcdef
# [both] HMAC key binding a CSRF token to its session. Distinct from COOKIE_SECRET on purpose (rotating one must
# not read as a change to the other). 32+ characters.
CSRF_SECRET=local-dev-only-csrf-secret-0123456789abcdefgh
# [API] Passwordless sign-in for local use and the e2e suites: POST /api/auth/test-login {"email": "<a seeded email>"}.
# Loads ONLY when this is `enabled` AND NODE_ENV is exactly `test` or `development`; the env schema refuses it when
# NODE_ENV=production. Unset it to sign in with Microsoft only.
IDENTITY_TEST_LOGIN=enabled

# ── Microsoft Entra ID sign-in (OPTIONAL locally — the test login above needs none of it) ────────────────────────
# To sign in with Microsoft locally, register a DEV app (a separate registration from production's) in a tenant you
# control: Web platform, redirect URI  http://localhost:5173/api/auth/callback/microsoft , delegated OpenID scopes
# only. The account that signs in must be on a staff domain (`qnsc.vn`, libs/modules/identity/.../staff-domains.ts).
# Required in production; README → "Sign in" has the steps.
# [both] tenant id (a UUID); [API] client id and client secret.
ENTRA_TENANT_ID=
ENTRA_CLIENT_ID=
ENTRA_CLIENT_SECRET=
# [API] The key the Cloudflare Pages Function signs the visitor's address with, verified by `clientIp()`. Leave empty
# locally (no Function in front: the address is the socket's). Required, >= 32 characters, in production.
EDGE_CLIENT_IP_SECRET=
# [API] Only while rotating: the previous key, still accepted for verification.
EDGE_CLIENT_IP_SECRET_PREVIOUS=

# ── Microsoft Graph (OPTIONAL — Intune device sync, shadow-IT scan, Secure Score, Entra account disable/revoke) ──
# [WORKER] The Graph app is a DIFFERENT registration from ENTRA_CLIENT_ID and has no client secret: a certificate
# (PEM, raw or base64) or, on k3s, a projected ServiceAccount token file. Leave GRAPH_CLIENT_ID empty and every Graph
# feature is skipped with one info log. Setup: docs/graph-worker-app.md. `pnpm start:dev` blanks these for the API.
GRAPH_CLIENT_ID=
GRAPH_CLIENT_CERTIFICATE=
GRAPH_FEDERATED_TOKEN_FILE=

# ── Cache (Valkey) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# [both] 6380 is the host port docker-compose.dev.yml maps (rova's stack owns 6379). Leave empty to disable the cache
# in local development. VALKEY_URL is the name infra and CI set; the `redis://` scheme is the wire protocol, not a vendor.
# The old name REDIS_URL still works for one more release (it logs a deprecation warning): rename it in your `.env`.
VALKEY_URL=redis://localhost:6380
REDIS_KEY_PREFIX=opshub:

# ── Object storage (S3 → LocalStack locally) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# [both] LocalStack is the compose stack's S3 on host port 4567 (rova's owns 4566). Its init hook
# (scripts/localstack/01-bootstrap.sh) creates the bucket below, with the CORS rules the SPA's direct PUT needs, and
# the compose healthcheck only passes once it exists.
#
# StorageService selects its backend from STORAGE_ENDPOINT. With it UNSET the SDK talks to real AWS S3 with the task
# role, which fails on a laptop (no credentials) and, worse, would reach a real bucket. `test`/`test` are
# LocalStack's fixed fake credentials; path-style addressing is required by LocalStack and R2 alike.
S3_FILES_BUCKET=opshub-files-dev
STORAGE_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4567
STORAGE_ACCESS_KEY_ID=test
STORAGE_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=test
STORAGE_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true
AWS_REGION=ap-southeast-1
# For the `aws` CLI and the bootstrap script only (the app reaches LocalStack through STORAGE_ENDPOINT above).
AWS_ENDPOINT_URL=http://localhost:4567
# Optional: a CDN base URL for downloads (overrides presigned S3 GET URLs), and the SQS queue of SES bounce verdicts.
# CDN_FILES_BASE_URL=
# SES_BOUNCE_QUEUE_URL=

# ── Observability ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
SERVICE_VERSION=dev
# [both] The DEPLOYMENT environment, distinct from NODE_ENV: stamped on every log line as `env` and on the OTel
# resource as deployment.environment.name. `local` keeps a laptop's output out of develop's and production's panels.
DEPLOYMENT_ENV=local
LOG_LEVEL=info
LOG_PRETTY=true
LOG_SQL=false
# [both] ON locally because the compose stack ships the collector that receives it (scripts/otel/collector.yaml):
# spans and metrics land in `docker logs opshub-otel`, and metrics are readable as Prometheus text with
# `curl -s localhost:8890/metrics`. Set false to run without the compose stack, or the SDK retries a dead socket.
OTEL_ENABLED=true
OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=opshub-api
OTEL_WORKER_SERVICE_NAME=opshub-worker
# 4319, not 4318 (rova's collector owns that); see the otel service in docker-compose.dev.yml.
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4319

# ── Email ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# [both] EMAIL_PROVIDER=dev logs the message to the console and sends nothing (the default; no setup at all).
#        ses    → AWS SES via the task role (deployed environments). Needs the domain verified in SES.
#        resend → Resend (set RESEND_API_KEY).
# Any non-dev provider refuses to boot without MAIL_FROM_EMAIL: mail from an address on a domain the deployment does
# not own fails SPF and DKIM at the recipient, which reads as silent non-delivery. The address below is the one
# develop uses, on our own domain; with the dev provider it is only shown in the log.
EMAIL_PROVIDER=dev
MAIL_FROM_NAME=OpsHub
MAIL_FROM_EMAIL=opshub-noreply@qnsc.vn
# Where replies go; unset means replies land on the no-reply sender.
MAIL_REPLY_TO=
# SES only: tags each send so a bounce or complaint can be tied back to the message.
SES_CONFIGURATION_SET=
RESEND_API_KEY=

# ── Webhooks ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A webhook delivery still `pending` after this many hours is never sent: the relay marks it `failed` ("expired:
# pending longer than N h") and keeps the row. Whole hours, default 24.
# WEBHOOK_DELIVERY_MAX_AGE_HOURS=24

# ── Shutdown (platform-runtime's enableGracefulShutdown; both processes) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# On SIGTERM/SIGINT: /readyz answers 503, keep serving for the endpoint-removal delay, drain HTTP, close the app
# (relays stop, then the pool and cache close), flush telemetry, exit. Keep the deadline below the platform's grace
# period (ECS stopTimeout / the chart's terminationGracePeriodSeconds, 30 s by default). Milliseconds.
# SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT_MS=25000
# Default 5000 inside a pod (KUBERNETES_SERVICE_HOST is set) and 0 elsewhere, so Ctrl-C on a laptop is immediate.
# SHUTDOWN_ENDPOINT_DELAY_MS=0

# ── Optional features ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# AI assistant: unset and the /ai endpoints answer 503.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# RATE_LIMIT_DEFAULT_LIMIT=200
# AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS=730
